  "query_preview": "..A cold upper low near the Four Corners will eject \ninto Colorado tonight, leaving our skies clear and cool. At the same \ntime, a cold front diving through the Great Plains will bring fairly \nrobust easterly gradients into our state. Most east winds will start \non Thursday, peaking early Friday and then dropping off heading into \nthe weekend. Although there will be some locally enhanced gusts over \n40 mph at times, much of the area should stay below criteria.\n\nThis front is the result of a highly-apmlifed pattern that will \nspawn a new cut-off low off the Baja Peninsula by Friday. This low \nwill hang around for a few days, potentially tapping into moisture \nfrom Hurricane Simon and Tropical Storm Trudy. Even if these systems \ndo not feed into the low, they are likely to induce a surge through \nthe Gulf of California.\n\nSounding familiar? Yes, we are entering another weekend with the \npotential for tropical moisture, followed by a cold front later next \nweek. This setup does not look quite as crazy as the last one, but \nstay in tune with your weather forecasts over the next few days.",
